
                               COMMANDS
                               ========

There are a lot of commands and I haven't got the willpower to write detailed
descriptions of all of them so you'll just have to try them out yourself
and play with the example code.
   There are 2 types of commands - interactive only and interactive/runtime
commands. Interactive only commands cannot be used in a program.

Interactive only
----------------

RUN
  Runs the program

CLEAR
  Deletes all variables.

NEW
  Deletes all variables and the current program.

DELETE <line number from> [TO <line number to>]
  Deletes the given program lines.
  
RENUM <start line number>
  This renumbers the program starting at the given start line number. It also
  uses this number as the line increment value.

LOAD <filename>
  Loads the given program into memory. Note than it does not automatically
  add .BAS onto the end.

SAVE <filename>
  Saves the current program out to the given file. As with LOAD it does not
  automatically add .BAS onto the end of the program name. 


Runtime
-------

REM
  Used for comments. 

LIST
  Lists the program. Normally it will indent loops and IF/FI unless the
  INDENT 0 command is used. This command will page if PAGING is on (which
  is the default). 

INDENT <number of chars>
  This sets the number of space characters to put in front of something when
  indenting it. If you set it to zero no indenting will happen. The default
  is 5 chars since this happens to look nice with cascading ELSE IF blocks.

END
  Causes the program to stop.

EXIT <code>
  Causes the intepreter process to exit with the given numeric code. 

DIM <array>(<size>)[,<array2>(<size>)[,....]]
  Dimension numeric or string arrays.

LET <var> = <value>
  This command is only included for the sake of completeness and does not
  actually need to be used since "<var> = <value>" can be used instead.

PRINT,WRITE [#<stream>],<data>,<more data>
  These commands both write to the given stream or stdout if no stream is
  given. The only difference is that PRINT automatically puts a newline at the
  end. A shortcut for the PRINT commands is "?".

GOTO <line number>
  The program will jump to the given line number if it exists. If not then
  an error is returned. 

GOSUB <line number>
  This makes the program jump to the given line number as a subroutine. So
  that when RETURN is called the program returns to the command immediately
  following the GOSUB (even if that command was on the same line).

  eg: 10 GOSUB 20: print "END": end
      20 print "SUB": return

RETURN 
  Returns from a subroutine as explained above. 

WHILE <expression>
WEND
  This loop type loops until the expression is false.

DO
UNTIL <expression>
  This loop type loops until the expression is true.

FOR <var> = <from value> TO <to value> [STEP <num>]
NEXT
  This loop type set the variable to the from value then on each subsequent
  loop increments by 1 (or the optional step value if given).
     Previously the step value was automaticaly calculated if not given so the 
  loop would always run (eg: FOR f = 10 to 1: NEXT - would run) but I've
  changed it to more conventional behaviour in that the loop will NOT run
  if end value is less than the start value and no stepping is given (or
  is positive) or the end value is more than the start value and stepping is 
  negative. However in these cases the variable will be set to the start value 
  before the programs jumps to the command following NEXT.
     Note than unlike a lot of other BASICs there is no variable name in the
  NEXT command because IMO its not required and adds complexity to the 
  intepreter for no advantage.

IF <expression> THEN <do something> FI
IF <expression> THEN <do something> ELSE <do something else> FI
  This differs from most old style line basics in that it allows multiple
  statements in an IF which requires the FI statement.

  eg: IF abc > 3 THEN
         print "YES"
         abc = 3
      FI

CHOOSE <expression>
  This is the head for the CHOOSE - CHOSEN block. The expression can return
  either a string or number but the case arguments must match the expression
  type in CHOOSE. These blocks can be nested.

  eg: 10 INPUT a
      20 CHOOSE a
      30    CASE 1: print 1: goto 60
      40    CASE 2: print 2: goto 60
      50    DEFAULT: print 999
      60 CHOSEN

CASE <expression>[,<expression>...]
   Used in CHOOSE - CHOSEN block. The expression type must match that of
   the CHOOSE. As in C the program will "fall through" to the next CASE
   statement if there is one since this is more useful (IMO , others may
   have other views) plus its easier to implement. If you wish to jump out
   of the CHOOSE - CHOSEN block after a CASE just use GOTO.

DEFAULT
   Optional in CHOOSE - CHOSEN block. The program will come here if it can't
   find a match CASE value.

CHOSEN
   Terminals a CHOOSE block.

DATA <data>[,<data>,<expression>,<data>,....]
RESTORE <line number>
READ <var1>[,<var2>[,...]]
  DATA stores data or expressions which are then read by READ. The restore
  set the line number from which to start READ reading from.

  eg: 10 DATA 123,"hello",a+1,"cruel",a+5
      20 DATA "wor" & a$,b+20,"there"
      30 RESTORE 10
      40 a=1: b=2: a$ = "ld"
      50 FOR i=1 TO 4: READ c,d$: PRINT c,",",d$: NEXT
   
PAUSE <milliseconds>
  Pause for the given number of milliseconds.

INPUT [#<stream>],<var>[,<var>...]
  This reads data off the given stream (stdin if not given) and into the
  variable(s). Reading for each variable is complete once a newline is 
  reached. Upon reaching EOF then input will return without setting the
  variable and the EOF() function will now return 1. However , if you try
  and read from the stream again an error will be returned.

CINPUT [#<stream>],<length>,<var>
  This does the same as above except it reads in a single character into
  the variable (or a single digit if not a string variable). This doesn't do
  any processing on the keys read in so this is useful for programs 
  requiring immediate keyboard interation such as games especially when
  coupled with the HASDATA() function.

OPEN <filename> TO READ/WRITE/APPEND/READWRITE AS #<stream>[,<permissions>]
  This opens the file and attaches it to the given stream. This can then
  be read and written to using INPUT and PRINT/WRITE. The default permissions
  when creating the file are 0644 (depending on your umask) unless you pass
  in the optional permissions argument. Obviously if you are simply accessing
  a pre-existing file these have no effect.

OPENDIR <dirname> AS #<stream>
  This opens a directory and allows the list of objects inside to be read
  using the INPUT command. Each INPUT will return the name of an object in
  the directory. The stream is closed as notmal using CLOSE. Note that you
  cannot use CINPUT on a stream opened using this command.

CLOSE #<stream>
  Closes an open stream.

SEEK #<stream>,<bytes>
SEEKSTART #<stream>,<bytes>
  SEEK moves the current pointer in the stream backwards or forwards from
  the current position by the given number of bytes whereas SEEKSTART moves
  the pointer from the start of the stream. Note that with streams opened
  with OPENDIR only SEEKSTART may be used and only with a value of zero which
  simply rewinds it to the start. This is due to the hopeless implementation
  of the POSIX seekdir() function which has to use specific values returned
  by telldir() for a given location meaning for every location we'd
  have to store in memory the values returned by telldir().

ON ERROR GOTO <line>
ON ERROR GOSUB <line>
ON ERROR BREAK
ON ERROR CONTINUE
  These commands determine what happens during an error. BREAK is the
  default and the program will stop. CONTINUE means the error is ignored
  and execution just continues. If is line are set then on an error the
  program jumps to the given line and last_error can be used to determine the
  error. Beware that recursion (GOSUB) or an infinite loop (GOTO) can occur if
  an error occurs in the place jumped to. These do not need to be reset after
  an error, they're permanent.

CLS [#<stream>]
  Clears the screen or writes the ANSI codes to whatever stream you give.

PEN <pen number>
  This sets the pen (foreground) colour. The number of pens available is
  defined by the num_pens system variable. Note than the ANSI control codes
  used in PRINT/WRITE to do the colours will be written out to files if
  PRINT/WRITE is used with a stream.

PAPER <paper number>
  This sets the paper (background) colour. The number of papers available is
  defined by the num_papers system variable. 

STYLE <style number>
  This sets the style. The number of styles is defined by the num_styles 
  system variable. Not all styles work on all terminals.

LOCATE [#<stream>],<x>,<y>
  This locates the cursor at the given screen x and y position. If x or y
  is offscreen then is is rounded up or down to the nearest valid screen
  position. If you give a stream it writes the ANSI code to the given stream.
  If its a file you can cat the file on the unix command line and see 
  animation.

SCROLL [#<stream>],<lines>
  This scrolls the terminal up or down the given number of lines. This doesn't
  work on all terminals (eg: Linux console). If the stream is given it writes
  the ANSI code to this. Scrolling is not supported by the Linux console.

SRAND <seed>
  Sets the seed for random numbers.

ECHO ON/OFF
  This turns keyboard echoing to the terminal on or off.

CURSOR ON/OFF
  This turns the terminal cursor on or off. This doesn't work properly on 
  the BSD console.

REMOVE <filename>
  This removes the directory or file (if possible).

RENAME <from filename>,<to filename>
  This renames the first filename to the second.

MKDIR <dirname>[,<permissions>]
  This creates a directory with the default permissions of 0755 unless you
  specify the file permissions option. Note however that the permissions
  are modified by your umask.
  eg: mkdir "tmp",644

CHMOD <filename>,<permissions>
  This allows you to change the permissions on a file or directory. The
  permissions are in octal format only (though you do not need a leading 
  zero). Unlike MKDIR these permissions are NOT modified by your umask.
  eg: chmod "wibble",5644

CD <path>
  This changes the current working directory.

DIR [<path>]
  This is an interactive command that prints out a directory listing.

BREAK ON/OFF
  If OFF then ^C will NOT stop the program. In fact there will be no way to stop
  the program interactively other than by killing the interpreter. Complemented
  by the -nobreak command line option.

PAGING ON/OFF
  This will switch paging in LIST on or off. To quit from the list when
  paging just press 'Q'.

SORT <array>
  This command will sort an array of numbers or strings into ascending order. 
  If you want descending values simply read the resulting sorted array from 
  the end backwards. If the array has dimensions greater than 1 then the sort
  affects minor indexes before major ones.
  eg: for DIM a(2,3) the sort will go 1,1 -> 1,2 -> 1,3 -> 2,1 -> 2,2 -> 2,3
 
